<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (The distribution of reported dengue case counts reveals a highly right-skewed distribution. A large proportion of observations are concentrated at low case numbers, while a small number of time periods exhibit extremely high case counts, representing outbreak events. This long right tail indicates substantial variability and the presence of extreme )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4 5]
>>
endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (values that can disproportionately influence statistical analyses. Such skewness violates the normality assumption required for many parametric methods and suggests heteroscedasticity where variability increases with the magnitude of cases. Therefore, the raw case data are not well suited for direct use in correlation or linear regression analysis without transformation.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 128 0 R
/A [743 0 R 0]
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (The histogram of log-transformed dengue case counts shows a markedly improved distribution compared to the original data. The transformation reduces the influence of extreme outbreak values and compresses the long right tail, resulting in a distribution that is more symmetric and closer to normality. Variability across observations appears more stable, indicating improved homoscedasticity. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9 10]
>>
endobj
255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (This transformation enhances the suitability of the data for parametric statistical techniques such as correlation analysis and linear regression, as it better satisfies underlying model assumptions. Consequently, the log\(cases + 1\) transformation was adopted for subsequent analyses involving continuous modeling of dengue incidence.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
